.\"
.\" Describes a driver written from a functional specification of the
.\" BCM2712 PM block.
.\"
.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt BCM2712_PM 4 aarch64
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bcm2712_pm
.Nd BCM2712 power management block: V3D power domain and reset
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following lines in the
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options FDT"
.Cd "device hwreset"
.Cd "device bcm2712_pm"
.Ed
.Pp
To load the driver as a module:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldload bcm2712_pm
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver gives the V3D GPU of the Broadcom BCM2712 its power domain and its
reset line.
Both belong to the PM block of the SoC, whose register window is also the
SoC's watchdog, reboot and power-off controller.
The driver takes the whole window and implements only the two things the
GPU needs:
.Bl -bullet
.It
reset 0, the V3D reset, as a reset line of the kernel's reset framework;
.It
power domain 1, the V3D domain, through functions of its own, declared in
.In arm64/broadcom/bcm2712/bcm2712_pm.h ,
as the kernel has no framework for power domains.
.El
.Pp
The numbers are the cells with which a consumer's device tree node names
them in its
.Va resets
and
.Va power-domains
properties.
Any other number is refused.
.Pp
On the BCM2712 both are the same thing: one bit, bit 6 of the register at
offset 0x304 of the window, which is an active-low reset of the GPU.
The domain is on while the bit is set and off while it is clear.
Asserting the reset clears the bit; deasserting it waits at least one
microsecond and sets it, so that an assertion followed by a deassertion is
a complete reset.
A reset leaves the bit set, that is the domain on, whatever it found.
.Pp
A consumer gets a handle for a power domain, enables and disables it, and
releases it.
The driver counts the enabled handles of a domain, switches the domain on
when the first is enabled and off when the last is disabled or released.
.Pp
The V3D domain has a parent domain, number 0.
Before the driver switches the V3D domain on it powers the parent on, and
after it has switched the V3D domain off it powers the parent off, with the
sequences and the register, at offset 0x10c, that the other SoCs of the
family have for it.
If the parent's power switch is closed already, nothing is written.
Otherwise the switch is closed, power good is waited for, isolation is
removed and memory repair is run; if power does not become good or the
repair does not finish, what was done is undone, the V3D domain stays off
and its consumer does not attach.
Powering the parent off opens the switch again.
A reset of the GPU does not touch the parent domain.
.Pp
The driver writes that one bit and the parent's power switch, and nothing
else; every write carries the password the block requires in its top byte.
The registers of the window that reboot or halt the system, or arm the
watchdog, are never written.
.Pp
The driver attaches before the devices of the default bus pass, so that
its consumers find it when they attach.
It refuses to detach while a power domain handle exists.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver supports the PM block described by a device tree node with the
compatible string:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
brcm,bcm2712-pm
.El
.Pp
The node's register window, named
.Dq pm
if its windows are named, must be at least 0x308 bytes long.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following read-only variables are available under
.Va dev.bcm2712_pm.%d .
Registers are shown in hexadecimal.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va reg_304
The register at offset 0x304, read when asked.
Bit 6 is set while the V3D domain is on.
.It Va reg_304_attach
The same register as it was read at attach, before the driver wrote
anything.
.It Va reg_10c
The register at offset 0x10c, the parent domain's power switch, read when
asked.
.It Va reg_10c_attach
The same register as it was read at attach.
.It Va grafx_v3d_users
The number of enabled handles of the V3D power domain.
The domain is on while it is not 0.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: cannot map the register window"
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: register window is 0x%jx bytes, 0x%x needed"
The device tree node does not describe the PM block as the driver needs it.
The driver does not attach.
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain: power did not become good: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain: memory repair did not finish: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
The parent domain could not be powered on.
The value is the register as the wait ended.
The V3D domain is not switched on.
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain power-on starts: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain power-on ends: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain power-on ends in a timeout: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain power-off starts: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: parent domain power-off ends: 0x10c = 0x%08x"
Logged at debug priority each time the V3D domain is switched.
.It "bcm2712_pm%d: 0x304 = 0x%08x, 0x10c = 0x%08x"
The two registers as found at attach.
Printed in a verbose boot.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fdt 4 ,
.Xr v3d 4 ,
.Xr kldload 8
.Sh CAVEATS
That the register at offset 0x10c is the parent domain's power switch on
the BCM2712, with the fields it has on the other SoCs of the family, is not
established.
.Pp
The driver owns the register window of the watchdog, reboot and power-off
controller and provides none of those functions.
.Pp
The other power domains and reset lines that the block's device tree
binding numbers are not implemented.
